# Hashes in CONTENT_MANIFEST are over bytes, and Git for Windows ships with
# core.autocrlf=true by default. Without this file a Windows clone rewrites every
# text file to CRLF, so content-manifest verification reports all 256 tracked
# files as DIFFERS -- and entrypoint.sh, copied into Linux containers, fails with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M". Pin LF and neither happens.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# PowerShell is CRLF-tolerant, so these stay LF with everything else for hashing.
